# Doc

`Doc` is a Markdown document: the content a person wrote, as opposed to the
metadata said about it. It is HQL's view of the CommonMark body that follows a
card's optional frontmatter block, plus the structure HyperMarkDown's hyper layer
adds on top of it.

An [[card-type|HmdCard]] *holds* a `Doc`; it is not one. A card is a header plus
a document, and `card.body` reaches through to `card.doc.body`. There is no
inheritance between the two types — see [[card-type]] for why.

Keeping `Doc` a type of its own is what makes that worth doing. A document need
not be a card: an embedded fragment, a constructed body and an ordinary `.md`
file that was never given a header are all documents, and none of them have a
place in a vault or a header to carry.

The split also matters because the two halves are open in different ways. A
header is open in its *shape*, which is why it is a [[data-type|Data]] tree. A
document is open in its *prose*, but its structure is small and fixed: a body
made of headings, anchored blocks, links and embeds. That structure can carry
real types.

Nothing on this card is implemented. The host adapter described in
[[hmd-integration]] would supply it from the existing HMD parser and resolver.

## Structure

| Type | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `Doc` | One authored Markdown document |
| `Section` | A heading and the content beneath it |
| `Block` | Anchored content addressable by name |
| `Link` | A reference from this document to a target |

A section runs from its heading to the next heading of equal or higher level —
the same rule HMD uses when expanding a heading fragment. Sections nest, so a
document's headings form a tree rather than a flat list. Whether `Doc` exposes
that tree, a flat sequence, or both is open.

```hql
doc.body      : Hmd
doc.sections  : Seq<Section>
doc.title     : String?
```

The example corpus ascribes `Hmd` to a card's body, which suggests `Doc` is the
document and `Hmd` is the marked-up content inside it; whether those are two
types or one name used twice is not settled. A title is optional because HMD does
not require a card to open with a heading, and because a title may equally come
from a card's header — which of the two wins needs a decision, and it is a
decision about cards rather than about documents.

## Addressing

HyperMarkDown addresses content at three granularities, and HQL should be able
to name all three:

```hql
[[alice]]           // the card
[[alice#Bio]]       // a heading fragment
[[alice#^claim]]    // an anchored block
```

A fragment reference is a reference to a part, so the natural reading is that a
reference carries the type of what it points at:

```hql
bio : Ref[Section] = [[alice#Bio]]
resolve(bio)
```

`resolve : Ref[T] -> T` is the conceptual signature. It can fail, and the failure
modes are HMD's own: a target that does not exist, a fragment missing from a card
that does, a name matching several cards, or a target escaping the namespace
root. HMD treats an unresolved link as a warning, deliberately — writing forward
to a card that has not been written yet is permitted, and the diagnostic report
is a to-do list. HQL must not turn that into a hard error by accident. How
failure is carried, and whether generic references use brackets or angles,
remain open.

Resolution itself belongs to the adapter, not to HQL. A bare name is resolved by
named imports, then the spine walk outward from the card's own directory, then
wildcard import origins, then autodiscovery — and ambiguity is an error with no
ranking rule. HQL consumes the outcome. It must preserve unresolved and ambiguous
references as outcomes rather than silently picking a card.

## Links

A link is lightweight structural connectivity, conceptually `Link(this, ref)`.
It belongs to the document, while the identity on either end of it belongs to a
card. It is not an edge-property container, and display text does not declare a
relationship type: `[[alice|Alice B.]]` links the same way `[[alice]]` does.

Links traverse in two directions. `uplinks` are the card's outgoing references;
`downlinks` are the references that point at it. A card can be its own uplink
and downlink. The older `backlinks` spelling is dropped because it named only
one half of the relation.

When a relationship needs properties, provenance, discussion or its own incoming
links, it should stop being a link and become a Relation Card — see
[[hql/knowledge]]. That is the boundary this card is defending: `Doc` stays
cheap, and meaning lives elsewhere.

## Embeds

An embed pulls another card, heading or block into this one:

```text
![[glossary/token#^definition]]
```

Embeds raise a question `Doc` has to answer explicitly: does a document's
content include what it transcluded? Both readings are useful. Rendering erases
the boundary, and the reader sees one continuous page. Analysis usually must not:
a word count, a link inventory or an evidence check should distinguish what this
card asserts from what it borrowed.

The current direction is that `Doc` preserves the boundary and expansion is a
separate operation, so that provenance survives. HMD's own limits apply to any
expansion — cycles are an error and nesting is bounded — and an embedded block
keeps its defining card as its origin.

## Doc is not text

Reducing a card to its source string would make HQL a text-search language over
`.hmd` files, which [[hql/core]] explicitly rejects. `Doc` is structure: a
pipeline selects sections, follows links and inspects anchors, and only reaches
for raw text where text is genuinely what is wanted.

The same applies in the other direction. Constructing a `Doc` produces a value;
it does not write a file. Persistence is an explicit effect, and rendering
belongs to the host — see the presenter vocabulary in [[hql/knowledge]].

Serialization back to `.hmd` is unresolved, and is not merely the inverse of
parsing: a round trip has to decide what happens to formatting the parser did
not keep, and whether expanded embeds collapse back into their embed syntax.

See [[card-type]] for the card that holds a document, [[data-type]] for the
header's tree type, [[pipes]] for composition and [[hql/design-status]] for open
decisions.
